# Normalize line endings to LF everywhere: in the repository and on checkout.
#
# Without this, a file edited on Windows by an editor that writes CRLF ends up with
# MIXED endings - some lines LF, some CRLF - and every later diff to that file is
# mostly line-ending noise rather than the change that was actually made. Twenty
# files in this tree were in that state.
#
# `eol=lf` rather than `text=auto`: `auto` would hand CRLF back out on a Windows
# checkout, which is the same fight again. The build, the scripts and the
# third-party sources all work with LF, so LF everywhere is the one setting that
# cannot produce a mixed file.
* text eol=lf

# The one exception, because the platform's own ending is the one that works: a
# .bat file with LF line endings is not guaranteed to run on Windows.
*.bat text eol=crlf